abstract: >-
  Long-term monitoring study of a DIY 12V 500Ah LiFePO₄ battery bank
  demonstrating "architectural immunity" — the principle that parallel-connected
  mixed-brand cells achieve monolithic behavior through topology rather than
  cell matching. The dataset spans 301 days across two instruments: a Shelly
  Plus Uni voltmeter (Oct 2025 – Jul 2026, voltage only, 10 mV quantisation) and,
  from July 2026, a purpose-built INA228 monitor on a 375 uOhm shunt sampling
  voltage and current every 2 seconds. It includes ~767,000 Shelly high-frequency
  samples, 1.79 million INA228 current samples, temperature correlation, full
  charge and discharge characterisation, and a paired cross-calibration of the two
  instruments. The study validates 99.3% usable capacity (397 Ah) and reports a
  directly measured quiescent drain of 7.4 mA — which, with the Shelly and DROK
  meter retired and the inverter off, is the monitor's own consumption — giving
  approximately 15 months to 80% SOC and replacing the 7-10 month projection that
  earlier releases inferred from voltage drift alone. Self-discharge plus internal
  BMS standby, neither of which crosses an external shunt, is bounded below
  0.9 %SOC per month at 95% confidence rather than measured; a full-to-full
  coulombic reconciliation is the designated next step. The dataset also
  documents the bank's only unplanned discharge: an 11.08-hour grid outage on
  2026-07-04 during which the bank never came within 340 mV of its first
  alarm, and which ended when the inverter's fuses failed on a coincident
  fridge-inrush and coffee-maker load of 3328 W against a 1500 W nameplate -
  a coincidence the 16-CT submeter shows recurring on 34 of 45 days.
